# Reading the results

## Step 1 — Bucket every respondent

Apply in order. First match wins.

| Segment | Rule | What it means |
|---|---|---|
| **Out of reach** | Q2 is 0–19% **and** Q3 is "No" or "technically possible but never" | Claude Code cannot touch their work today. Not a persuasion problem, and excluding them isn't a judgement on them. |
| **Covered** | Q5 is 70%+ **and** Q7 is "None" or "1–2" **and** Q10 includes "I'd keep my current setup" | Their existing tool genuinely does the job. A seat here produces a dormant licence and a bad adoption statistic. |
| **Core** | Q2 is 60%+ **and** Q7 is "3–5" or more **and** Q10 names at least one tool they'd drop | Wave-one cohort. Real IDE time, agent-shaped work, stated willingness to displace something. |
| **Convertible** | Meets Core on Q2 and Q7, but Q9 has fewer than 3 ticks **or** Q10 says "none" | The work fits; the understanding doesn't yet. These convert with a demo, not a licence. |
| **Unclear** | Anything else | Read Q12b by hand. Small group, and the free text usually settles it. |

**Your headline number is Core plus Convertible.** That's the addressable population — not the excitement score.

## Step 2 — Turn Block 5 into a written charter *before* rollout

This is the part most rollouts skip, and it's why they end up arguable six months later. Take five things straight out of the survey and put them on one page that goes out with the announcement:

1. **The metrics** — the top 3 from Q15, aggregated. These are what success means. Nothing else gets substituted later.
2. **The bar** — the median answer to Q16, stated as a per-person weekly target. Watch the tail here: if a meaningful group picks "no amount would justify losing what I have," that's a resistance cluster to talk to directly, not a number to average away.
3. **The evidence** — the top 2 from Q17, which tells you what to instrument on day one. If "before-and-after timings" ranks high, you need those timings *now*, before anyone gets a licence. A baseline you didn't capture cannot be reconstructed afterwards.
4. **The floor** — the top items from Q18. These are acceptance criteria for the switch itself, not nice-to-haves. If inline completion ranks high, be honest that an agent is a different product mode, and decide deliberately whether Copilot goes away entirely or the two run side by side for a period.
5. **The exit** — the top 2 from Q19, written down as the conditions under which you'd stop or roll back. Naming these in advance is what makes the whole exercise credible; a programme that can't fail can't succeed either.

## Step 3 — Three cross-checks

**Does excitement survive a trade-off?** Compare Q13 against Q11. People scoring 8+ who then pick "I wouldn't make that trade" are giving you enthusiasm without commitment. That gap is the most honest number in the survey.

**Is the incumbent actually the constraint?** Cross Q4 with Q5 and Q6. High reported coverage plus heavy ticking of "can't run the code" and "loses the thread" means the tool solves small problems well and has stopped being asked big ones. That reads as satisfaction until you look at Q7.

**Is Q14 believable?** It's self-reported, so treat it as an expectation to test rather than a measurement. Its real value is as a before-number: ask it again at 90 days and the delta is your ROI figure. Pair it with one hard metric from git history so the whole case doesn't rest on people's estimates of their own time.

## Step 4 — What to report, in this order

1. **Addressable population** — Core + Convertible, as a headcount
2. **Displacement** — % of Core naming a tool in Q10, and which came up most
3. **Coverage gap** — % of all respondents under 70% on Q5
4. **Success charter** — the five items from Step 2, one page
5. **Excitement** — Q13 mean, last, explicitly labelled as sentiment rather than demand

With under ~25 responses, use counts rather than percentages throughout. Percentages on a small sample invite people to argue with the sample instead of the finding.
